// BLAKE3, bound to the official C implementation.
//
// There is no `dependencies` block because BLAKE3's C depends on nothing a package manager could
// fetch -- it includes `<assert.h>`, `<stdbool.h>`, `<stddef.h>`, `<stdint.h>` and `<string.h>` and
// that is the whole list. The five files it needs are carried in this package.
//
// This is the block a *consumer* writes to reach this package:
//
//     dependencies {
//       blake3 { git = "github.com/sysl-lang/blake3", version = "0.1.0" }
//     }
//
// The coordinate is identity rather than a URL, so it carries no `https://`, and the version is the
// tag `v0.1.0` on this repository.

package {
  name    = "blake3"
  version = "0.1.0"

  // The compiler this was built and verified against, rather than a probed floor. Nothing here
  // reaches for a recent language feature; the number says what was actually run.
  sysl = "0.0.77"
}

// **There is deliberately no `requires` block.** A capability clause says what a package asks of
// *sysl*, and BLAKE3 hashes buffers it was handed: it opens no file, reads no clock, and calls no
// allocator -- there is no `malloc`, `calloc` or `free` in any of the three `.c` files carried here,
// which is what makes the caller-owned arrangement below possible at all.
//
// **That is not a claim that it builds for a bare target, and here it does not.** `blake3_impl.h`
// includes `<assert.h>` and `<string.h>`, and clang gives a freestanding target neither, so
// `--target thumb-freestanding` fails on the include. Any target with a libc is fine, and that covers
// the interesting ones: the Pico SDK's newlib, Zephyr, and a FreeRTOS application. The sibling `sha3`
// package is the one that genuinely needs nothing.
//
// **What is NOT here is a `@link` directive** -- the implementation is compiled from the `.c` carried
// in this package, so there is no external library for the linker to be given.

// What BLAKE3's own C is compiled with.
//
// **BLAKE3 ships one portable implementation and several vectorised ones, and the build has to say
// which.** Upstream expects a build script to detect the CPU and hand the right files to the
// compiler; sysl compiles a package's C declaratively and runs no build script, so the choice is made
// here instead -- portable everywhere, and only the three portable `.c` files vendored.
//
// The four `NO_` macros switch off the x86 paths, whose implementations live in files this package
// does not carry. Without them `blake3_dispatch.c` refers to `blake3_hash_many_sse2` and friends and
// the link fails.
//
// **`BLAKE3_USE_NEON` is the one that is easy to miss, and it is the one that bites on this
// machine.** It is not an x86 macro and none of the four covers it: `blake3_impl.h` autodetects it to
// **1** on any aarch64 target -- a Mac, a phone, a Pi -- so a build carrying only the four above
// links against a `blake3_hash_many_neon` that is not here.
//
// It is set to 0 rather than answered by vendoring `blake3_neon.c`, because that file includes
// `<arm_neon.h>` unconditionally and guards none of its body: carrying it would break every target
// without NEON, which includes the RP2350 this organisation builds for. The cost is throughput on
// large inputs on ARM, and the alternative is a package that builds in fewer places.
defines {
  "sh/sysl/blake3/c/{blake3,blake3_dispatch,blake3_portable}.c" {
    BLAKE3_NO_SSE2   = true
    BLAKE3_NO_SSE41  = true
    BLAKE3_NO_AVX2   = true
    BLAKE3_NO_AVX512 = true
    BLAKE3_USE_NEON  = 0
  }
}
